WEBVTT

00:36.428 --> 00:38.395
Ah, this hunting trip
is long overdue.

00:38.430 --> 00:40.798
Yeah, I mean, it's just too bad
that all the-the animals

00:40.832 --> 00:42.533
are so spread out, you know?

00:42.567 --> 00:44.527
I mean, wouldn't it
be awesome if-if they were all

00:44.536 --> 00:46.036
grouped together,
like in a cafeteria,

00:46.071 --> 00:48.172
and you could just go in there
with a trench coat

00:48.206 --> 00:49.406
and just waste them?

00:49.441 --> 00:52.042
Well, maybe I can stir up
some quail or pheasant

00:52.077 --> 00:54.311
with my patented
bird calls.

00:54.346 --> 00:56.046
Birds! Quail! Pheasant!

00:56.081 --> 00:57.614
Come on out!

00:57.649 --> 01:01.552
I'm a bird of the opposite sex
who's interested in mating!

01:01.586 --> 01:03.287
Did you hear that?

01:03.321 --> 01:04.655
We're gonna get laid!

01:04.689 --> 01:06.490
Billy, Billy, come on.

01:06.524 --> 01:08.225
Don't be stupid.

01:20.271 --> 01:22.139
Should've taken
the shot, fat-ass.

01:28.179 --> 01:30.414
Hey, Peter, you got
your safety on, right?

01:30.448 --> 01:31.682
Yeah, of course.

01:31.716 --> 01:33.350
Okay, 'cause your gun is
pointing right at me.

01:33.385 --> 01:34.551
Well, you want me
to pull the trigger

01:34.586 --> 01:35.919
and prove it to you?

01:35.954 --> 01:37.755
No, I just want to make
sure your safety's on.

01:37.756 --> 01:38.989
There. See, Quagmire?

01:39.023 --> 01:39.990
Safety's on.

01:40.024 --> 01:41.992
Now this is a gun
without a safety.

01:43.328 --> 01:44.368
Quite the difference, huh?

01:44.396 --> 01:45.896
Damn it, Peter,
you son of a bitch!

01:45.930 --> 01:46.897
You shot me!

01:46.931 --> 01:48.132
Oh, my God, Peter!

01:48.166 --> 01:50.134
That might be the dumbest
thing you've ever done.

01:50.168 --> 01:51.735
No, the dumbest
thing I ever did

01:51.770 --> 01:53.604
was open that
can of whup-ass.

01:58.410 --> 02:00.110
Lois, why did you buy this?!

02:00.145 --> 02:02.579
It was on sale!

02:06.451 --> 02:10.220
A writer who inherits
a magic typewriter

02:10.255 --> 02:12.556
that writes for him,

02:12.590 --> 02:16.527
but then it turns out
the typewriter is... racist?

02:16.561 --> 02:18.328
Totally makes sense.

02:18.363 --> 02:20.330
Older technology,
older world view.

02:20.365 --> 02:22.299
And that is a good day.

02:22.333 --> 02:23.967
I earned some banana bread.

02:25.770 --> 02:26.737
Oh, crap.

02:26.771 --> 02:27.838
It's Dylan.

02:27.872 --> 02:29.606
Dylan? Your son?

02:29.641 --> 02:31.675
I haven't heard you
mention him in ages.

02:31.709 --> 02:35.078
Yeah, I-I decided to step away
from the whole situation.

02:35.113 --> 02:37.214
The last thing he needs
is a father who's,

02:37.248 --> 02:39.383
you know, sometimes
there, sometimes not.

02:39.417 --> 02:41.819
Really? When-when was the part
when you were there?

02:41.853 --> 02:43.287
Hey, if I
click "ignore,"

02:43.321 --> 02:45.055
will he know that I'm
sending him to voice mail?

02:45.089 --> 02:46.089
Hey, Dylan!

02:46.124 --> 02:47.558
Hey, it's Stewie!

02:47.592 --> 02:49.593
Yeah, I'm right here
with your dad!

02:50.862 --> 02:52.729
Hey... hey, Dylan.

02:52.764 --> 02:53.864
Hey, Dad!

02:53.898 --> 02:55.298
I know we haven't
talked in a while,

02:55.300 --> 02:57.601
but I'm calling because
I'm on my way to Quahog.

02:57.635 --> 02:58.902
Oh, really?

02:58.937 --> 03:00.571
W-When are you
getting here?

03:00.605 --> 03:01.772
Tuesday...
Oh, don't say Tuesday!

03:01.806 --> 03:04.074
Aw, aw, I'm at the...
I'm at the...

03:04.108 --> 03:05.909
I'm out at
the project all day.

03:05.944 --> 03:07.911
Aw, hands on,
indispensable.

03:07.946 --> 03:10.347
They-they need me there,
so, wow.

03:10.381 --> 03:12.082
Rats. Bummer.

03:12.116 --> 03:14.451
I can cover for you
at the project, Brian!

03:14.486 --> 03:15.519
Go see your boy!

03:15.553 --> 03:16.753
Dad, who's your coworker?

03:16.788 --> 03:17.788
He sounds nice.

03:17.822 --> 03:19.223
He's widely disliked
here, but, uh...

03:19.257 --> 03:20.958
I'll meet you Tuesday
at the coffee shop.

03:20.992 --> 03:22.793
Sounds great.
I'll see you then.

03:22.827 --> 03:24.795
Okay, b-bye.

03:24.829 --> 03:26.530
See? Don't you feel better?

03:26.564 --> 03:27.865
You're gonna go
to that lunch,

03:27.899 --> 03:29.266
you're gonna reconnect
with your son.

03:29.300 --> 03:30.267
It'll be great.

03:30.301 --> 03:31.668
I don't know, Stewie.

03:31.703 --> 03:33.270
Trust me, you should
take my advice.

03:33.304 --> 03:35.138
I mean, I'm the guy
who told Michael Chiklis

03:35.173 --> 03:36.740
to wear tight shirts.

03:36.774 --> 03:38.242
I'll tell you
anything you want,

03:38.276 --> 03:39.910
just get your boob
out of my face!

03:39.944 --> 03:42.513
You know the difference
between you and me, dirtbag?

03:42.547 --> 03:46.517
No matter how muscular I get,
I will never be muscular.

03:50.822 --> 03:52.556
Hey, Quagmire,
what's with the cast?

03:52.590 --> 03:55.192
Did you hurt your arm after
I shot you in the arm?

03:55.226 --> 03:56.994
The shooting was
the injury, Peter!

03:57.028 --> 03:59.530
Look, I already said I was
sorry, like, a hundred times.

03:59.564 --> 04:02.065
Oh, and before I forget,
I want my Huey Lewis CD back.

04:02.100 --> 04:03.333
Well, I want a new drug.

04:03.368 --> 04:04.701
Okay, at least
you've been using it.

04:04.736 --> 04:05.869
But I want
it back, Peter.

04:05.904 --> 04:06.870
This is it.
Ooh-ah.

04:06.905 --> 04:08.226
Damn it, Peter,
just give it back.

04:08.239 --> 04:10.507
I can't give it back.
I lost it in a poker game.

04:10.542 --> 04:12.342
What?
I didn't think he could beat a two.

04:12.377 --> 04:13.677
Where the hell
do you get off

04:13.711 --> 04:15.312
betting my CD
in a poker game?

04:15.346 --> 04:16.880
I don't think
I like your tone.

04:16.915 --> 04:19.883
W-What? You're-you're saying
I'm being the unreasonable one?!

04:19.918 --> 04:21.385
Jeez, calm down,
you guys.

04:21.419 --> 04:22.586
No, no, Joe, Joe, Joe,

04:22.620 --> 04:24.054
it's time somebody
said this to him.

04:24.088 --> 04:26.590
Peter Griffin,
you are an idiot!

04:26.624 --> 04:29.326
You can't even walk
and chew gum at the same time.

04:29.360 --> 04:31.295
Peter, come on!

04:31.329 --> 04:32.362
Walk over here.

04:33.598 --> 04:34.865
I'm doing something.

04:34.899 --> 04:37.134
One thing at a time.

04:37.168 --> 04:38.302
Damn it, Peter, that's it!

04:38.336 --> 04:40.103
I am done!
I'm done with you!

04:40.138 --> 04:41.338
I'm done with
all your crap!

04:41.372 --> 04:42.873
I never want to
speak to you again!

04:42.907 --> 04:44.474
This friendship is over!

04:44.509 --> 04:45.475
Well, fine!

04:45.510 --> 04:46.677
I don't need your friendship!

04:46.711 --> 04:47.945
I got other friends.

04:47.979 --> 04:49.846
I got my silent movie friends.

05:16.174 --> 05:18.175
Coming up, we'll meet
a Filipino man

05:18.209 --> 05:20.310
who actually has
a grown-up haircut.

05:20.345 --> 05:22.079
Hey, what are you
still doing here?

05:22.113 --> 05:24.014
Aren't you supposed to be
having lunch with Dylan?

05:24.048 --> 05:26.088
Yeah, you know, Stewie,
I've been thinking about it.

05:26.117 --> 05:28.085
And I haven't seen
him in years,

05:28.119 --> 05:30.220
and at this point,
I-I just don't see

05:30.254 --> 05:31.722
what could possibly
be gained

05:31.756 --> 05:32.990
from me getting
together with him.

05:33.024 --> 05:35.726
In entertainment news,
Hollywood comes to Quahog

05:35.760 --> 05:37.694
in the form of a new
Disney Channel show

05:37.729 --> 05:39.529
<i>called Parent Boppers,</i>

05:39.564 --> 05:41.198
about a group of
teen detectives.

05:41.232 --> 05:43.734
The show, set and filmed
right here in Rhode Island,

05:43.768 --> 05:47.571
stars local teen and
Irish trash Dylan Flannigan.

05:47.605 --> 05:49.039
Brian, did you hear that?

05:49.073 --> 05:51.341
Your son's gonna be a TV star!

05:51.376 --> 05:52.976
Brian?

05:56.147 --> 05:57.581
He's off to lunch, Meg.

05:57.615 --> 05:59.850
Oh, that dog o' mine!

05:59.884 --> 06:01.284
That's Mike Drunkbeater.

06:01.319 --> 06:02.552
Look it up on the Internet.

06:12.456 --> 06:16.092
Wow, my son Dylan
on a TV show.

06:16.126 --> 06:18.160
Funny how life
turns out, isn't it?

06:18.195 --> 06:20.396
You remember that weekend
we watched TV together?

06:20.430 --> 06:24.066
Boy, little did I know
how much that was gonna rub off.

06:24.101 --> 06:25.534
Did we watch
TV together?

06:25.569 --> 06:27.703
Oh, probably.
But, wow, huh?

06:27.737 --> 06:29.105
And-and you know what's weird?

06:29.139 --> 06:31.674
You're on a show...
I'm a writer.

06:31.708 --> 06:33.943
It's-it's almost like
the universe is trying to...

06:33.977 --> 06:35.444
...I don't know,

06:35.479 --> 06:37.446
make us work together
or something, I don't know.

06:37.481 --> 06:40.182
You... you want
a job on my show?

06:40.217 --> 06:41.951
Hey, hey, don't put
the universe's words

06:41.985 --> 06:43.185
in my mouth, okay?

06:43.220 --> 06:44.687
I don't know, Dad.

06:44.721 --> 06:46.622
I've-I've barely seen you
in the past few years.

06:46.656 --> 06:48.491
So, what better way
to reconnect

06:48.525 --> 06:50.159
than by working
together every day?

06:50.193 --> 06:51.727
Well, I suppose
I could talk to them,

06:51.761 --> 06:52.895
see if they'd
go for it.

06:52.929 --> 06:53.896
Why wouldn't they?

06:53.930 --> 06:55.131
I-I mean,
it wouldn't be

06:55.165 --> 06:56.966
the craziest idea
in show business.

06:57.000 --> 06:57.967
When I grow up,

06:58.001 --> 06:59.835
I want to stick my head
through a circle

06:59.870 --> 07:01.804
at the beginning
of movies and roar.

07:03.940 --> 07:06.876
What's up now, bitches?!

07:10.313 --> 07:12.815
<i>We now return to
Saving Private Ryan,</i>

07:12.849 --> 07:14.550
brought to you without
commercial interruption

07:14.584 --> 07:16.018
by Mazda.

07:23.126 --> 07:26.996
Shh, shh, shh,
shh, shh, shh.

07:28.832 --> 07:30.866
Zoom zoom.

07:36.306 --> 07:38.574
Hey, Jerome, can you
clear out my booth?

07:38.608 --> 07:40.876
Looks like somebody
left their trash here.

07:40.911 --> 07:43.145
Jerome, can you tell
that

07:43.180 --> 07:44.480
to  get
the hell out of here

07:44.514 --> 07:46.115
before I
kick him in the face?

07:46.149 --> 07:48.651
Peter, why don't you just sit
in that booth over there?

07:48.685 --> 07:51.253
Oh, man, that's where the
after-work secretaries sit.

07:53.156 --> 07:55.090
What about Mr. Demerson?
Is he single?

07:55.125 --> 07:56.659
I... I-I don't know.

07:56.693 --> 07:58.427
What about Mr. Adelaide?
Is he single?

07:58.461 --> 07:59.962
I don't know
who that is.

07:59.996 --> 08:01.764
Well, who do you know
who is single?

08:01.798 --> 08:03.766
Uh, I'm assuming you.

08:03.800 --> 08:05.434
Oh-oh, hey, look! Cake!

08:08.338 --> 08:10.372
Hey, Joe!
Hey, Joe, I'm over here!

08:10.407 --> 08:11.607
Joe! Joe!
Come sit with me!

08:11.641 --> 08:13.108
Come on, you guys.

08:13.143 --> 08:14.843
Joe, I got a box of
salt water taffies,

08:14.878 --> 08:16.011
and you can have two.

08:16.046 --> 08:17.179
Joe, I got
a deck of cards

08:17.214 --> 08:18.514
you can put in your
wheels to look cool.

08:18.548 --> 08:20.616
Joe, I'll do the wavy
stomach for you.

08:20.650 --> 08:22.785
Looks like a real wave.

08:22.819 --> 08:24.553
Joe, I can scream
your name real loud!

08:24.588 --> 08:27.122
Joe! Joe! Joe! Joe!

08:27.157 --> 08:28.257
I can yell it louder!

08:28.291 --> 08:30.793
Joe! Joe! Joe!

08:30.827 --> 08:34.263
Joe! Joe! Joe! Joe!
Joe! Joe! Joe! Joe! Joe!

08:34.297 --> 08:36.198
What the hell
is going on here?!

08:36.233 --> 08:38.000
I'm in there trying
to take a leak!

08:38.034 --> 08:39.568
What, you think
I'm here to amuse you?

08:39.603 --> 08:41.770
You made me dribble
on my Bruno Maglis

08:41.805 --> 08:43.806
turning to see
who's calling my name!

08:43.840 --> 08:45.741
Joe Pesci thought we were
talking to him

08:45.775 --> 08:47.409
'cause his name is Joe.

08:47.444 --> 08:50.279
Oh, you don't know who
Joe Pesci is, 'cause you're 14.

08:50.313 --> 08:53.983
You see, in the '90s,
this was a tough guy.

08:59.789 --> 09:01.724
Hey, multiracial
group of friends,

09:01.758 --> 09:03.125
you want to hang out?

09:03.159 --> 09:04.793
Cut! That's a five.

09:04.828 --> 09:07.129
Tell my assistant I fell asleep
with a condom on again,

09:07.163 --> 09:09.031
and to bring scissors
to my trailer.

09:11.468 --> 09:13.402
All right, guys,
before we begin today,

09:13.436 --> 09:16.438
I want to introduce our newest
staff writer, Brian Griffin.

09:16.473 --> 09:17.473
Hi, guys.

09:17.507 --> 09:19.308
Yes, I am that Brian Griffin.

09:19.342 --> 09:22.444
You probably have my novel,
but just so you know, in here,

09:22.479 --> 09:24.580
I'm just one of
the guys, all right?

09:24.614 --> 09:26.548
You have my novel, right?

09:26.583 --> 09:27.750
Nope? No?

09:27.784 --> 09:29.385
You got it, though.
Nope?

09:29.419 --> 09:30.486
You got it?

09:30.520 --> 09:32.354
Nope, nobody's got it.

09:32.389 --> 09:33.689
All right.

09:33.723 --> 09:35.457
Okay, so we got to
come up with a story

09:35.492 --> 09:36.458
for next week's episode.

09:36.493 --> 09:37.693
How about we do one

09:37.727 --> 09:39.194
where DJ gets his
driver's license?

09:39.229 --> 09:40.796
That could work.
I don't think we've done that.

09:40.830 --> 09:41.797
Love that idea.

09:41.831 --> 09:42.998
No, no, no.

09:43.033 --> 09:45.034
Guys, guys, come on,
where's the STWISTUSION?

09:46.236 --> 09:47.970
Really?
Really?

09:48.004 --> 09:49.605
I'm the only guy here
who's heard of that?

09:49.639 --> 09:52.641
Stakes, twists, conclusion--
STWISTUSION.

09:52.676 --> 09:54.209
That sounds made up.

09:54.244 --> 09:56.378
Let me ask you something:
are you afraid right now?

09:56.413 --> 09:57.713
What? No.

09:57.747 --> 09:58.914
That's what's wrong.

09:58.948 --> 10:00.516
You should be, you know?

10:00.550 --> 10:01.784
Art is scary.

10:01.818 --> 10:02.885
This is writing, man.

10:02.919 --> 10:04.253
The viewer wants to be raped.

10:04.287 --> 10:07.089
Are you saying my nine-year-old
daughter wants to be raped?

10:07.123 --> 10:09.024
Now you're asking
the right questions.

10:09.059 --> 10:11.960
Now you're being creative,
thinking outside the box.

10:11.995 --> 10:14.763
Take what you think you know
and go a different way with it.

10:14.798 --> 10:17.399
<i>Like-like, for instance,
what if the daughter in Taken</i>

10:17.434 --> 10:19.435
was Zooey Deschanel?

10:19.469 --> 10:21.704
Send me her head.

10:25.275 --> 10:27.576
Peter, Quagmire, I've
called you here today

10:27.610 --> 10:29.278
because this
has got to stop.

10:29.312 --> 10:31.580
The petty vying
for my attention,

10:31.614 --> 10:34.550
the late-night phone
calls, the attack ads.

10:34.584 --> 10:36.552
<i>Glenn Quagmire claims to be</i>

10:36.586 --> 10:38.020
<i>Joe Swanson's good friend.</i>

10:38.054 --> 10:41.390
<i>But would a "good friend"
not return a leaf blower</i>

10:41.424 --> 10:44.293
<i>for four months
during the leafy season?</i>

10:44.327 --> 10:45.461
I'm Peter Griffin,

10:45.495 --> 10:47.763
and I broke into a news station
to make this.

10:47.797 --> 10:49.098
Hey, who's that guy?

10:51.968 --> 10:55.003
Well, look, you guys have
given me no alternative.

10:55.038 --> 10:56.638
I have to choose
one of you.

10:56.673 --> 10:57.740
So I choose Quagmire.

10:57.774 --> 10:59.274
What?!
Suck it!

10:59.309 --> 11:00.509
I'm sorry, Peter,

11:00.543 --> 11:01.977
it's just that
Quagmire's been there

11:02.011 --> 11:03.645
for me in some
pretty dark days.

11:03.680 --> 11:06.148
And he's been a wonderful
godfather to Susie.

11:07.217 --> 11:08.984
There she is,
my little Susie-woozie!

11:10.520 --> 11:13.088
I love Susie more!
Come here, Susie!

11:13.123 --> 11:15.591
Uncle Peter's gonna
give you a raspberry.

11:17.627 --> 11:20.763
Peter, that's not
where you do a raspberry.

11:20.797 --> 11:23.165
I closed my eyes too early.

11:23.199 --> 11:25.134
I think it's best
if you leave.

11:25.168 --> 11:26.468
Oh, yeah?

11:26.503 --> 11:28.737
Well, maybe I'll hang out
with my godson, Kevin.

11:28.772 --> 11:30.305
Get out!

11:30.340 --> 11:32.441
He was masturbating
with a knife.

11:36.479 --> 11:38.347
We're rolling!

11:38.381 --> 11:41.483
My stupid parents want me
to wear a bike helmet.

11:41.518 --> 11:43.085
Parents are lame.

11:43.119 --> 11:45.120
Hold on, other kids.
Maybe they just recognize

11:45.155 --> 11:47.389
that nothing can protect us
from the tedium

11:47.424 --> 11:48.891
of our mundane lives.

11:48.925 --> 11:51.393
Aren't we all on bikes
without helmets?

11:51.428 --> 11:54.129
Sometimes I just want
to put this gun in my mouth.

11:54.164 --> 11:57.266
Cut! Your line is
"That's right, Cameron."

11:57.300 --> 11:59.268
I know. My dad wrote
that one for me.

11:59.302 --> 12:00.469
Again with this?

12:00.503 --> 12:02.137
Yesterday, you had
Dakota give herself

12:02.172 --> 12:05.040
an abdomen-punch abortion during
her volleyball tournament.

12:05.074 --> 12:07.009
Hey, seventh grade
can be hell.

12:07.043 --> 12:08.177
Brian, you're fired.

12:08.211 --> 12:09.178
Wait, what?!

12:09.212 --> 12:10.612
Sorry, Brian,
but you're done.

12:10.647 --> 12:13.582
Oh, come on! You can't fire a
writer in the middle of a show!

12:13.616 --> 12:15.017
That would be like
a doctor leaving

12:15.051 --> 12:16.351
in the middle
of a surgery!

12:17.554 --> 12:18.987
Help!

12:19.022 --> 12:21.557
I'm supposed to have surgery,
but my doctor disappeared.

12:21.591 --> 12:24.827
Okay, Josh, this is the shot
you've been waiting for.

12:27.664 --> 12:29.398
Okay, Derek,
this is the shot

12:29.432 --> 12:31.133
you've been waiting for.

12:33.036 --> 12:34.403
Dylan, Dylan,
there you are!

12:34.437 --> 12:36.004
Listen, you can't
let them fire me!

12:36.039 --> 12:37.339
You're the star
of the show!

12:37.373 --> 12:39.908
Dad, believe me, I want
you to work here with me,

12:39.943 --> 12:41.677
but you can't keep
doing things like that.

12:41.711 --> 12:43.145
And they know
you took those cases

12:43.179 --> 12:44.580
of Diet Coke
home last night.

12:44.614 --> 12:46.215
I thought they were
anybody's Diet Coke!

12:46.249 --> 12:47.716
They're for work!
I work at home!

12:47.750 --> 12:49.985
Oh, hey, by the way, are we all
getting those backpacks

12:50.019 --> 12:51.587
with the name
of the show on them?

12:51.621 --> 12:52.921
You know what, Dad?

12:52.956 --> 12:54.636
I'm starting to think
that you've just been

12:54.657 --> 12:56.725
using me to advance
your career!

12:56.759 --> 12:59.027
God! You're
a terrible father!

12:59.062 --> 13:01.096
I never want to
see you again!

13:03.800 --> 13:05.100
My God, he's right.

13:05.134 --> 13:08.237
<i>I am a terrible father.</i>

13:51.466 --> 13:53.334
Brian, do you
know where...

13:53.368 --> 13:55.336
What the hell
are you drinking?

13:55.370 --> 13:57.271
Is that Rite Aid-
brand whiskey?

13:58.640 --> 14:01.909
Stewie, I just feel so awful
about what happened with Dylan.

14:01.943 --> 14:04.578
I mean, every terrible thing
he said about me is true.

14:04.613 --> 14:06.313
Well, you're
a terrible guy, Bri.

14:06.348 --> 14:09.450
I think being a paid writer
again just went to my head.

14:09.484 --> 14:12.486
I mean, I just... I can't
believe I was so selfish.

14:12.520 --> 14:15.055
It's-it's like Dylan just held
a mirror up to me,

14:15.090 --> 14:18.392
and I could finally see how
poorly I'd been treating him.

14:18.426 --> 14:21.362
I guess I didn't realize how
lucky I was to have Dylan back

14:21.396 --> 14:24.598
in my life until I lost him
all over again.

14:24.633 --> 14:25.933
Well, maybe you
should just apologize.

14:25.967 --> 14:27.101
I tried to.

14:27.135 --> 14:29.904
I mean, I texted him
"S-R-Y frowny face."

14:29.938 --> 14:33.073
I mean, he knows that I'm sorry
and that I'm sad.

14:34.442 --> 14:36.777
What are you guys
doing up so early?

14:36.811 --> 14:37.878
What-what are we...

14:37.913 --> 14:39.246
Where have you been?

14:39.281 --> 14:42.283
Well, I certainly wasn't
at the Shriners' convention.

14:44.486 --> 14:45.753
You know what
I think, Brian?

14:45.787 --> 14:47.688
I think you've got to
get down to that set

14:47.722 --> 14:49.857
and apologize
face-to-face.

14:49.891 --> 14:51.325
You're right.

14:51.359 --> 14:54.495
I just... I hope he's willing
to hear me out, you know?

14:54.529 --> 14:56.397
I'm the worst father ever.

14:56.431 --> 14:58.799
I'm even worse than
Marvin Gaye's dad.

14:58.833 --> 15:00.401
Happy Father's Day, Dad.

15:00.435 --> 15:03.671
Marvin, if this another
tie, I'm gonna kill you.

15:13.181 --> 15:16.250
Well, looks like it's just
you and me, shadow.

15:18.553 --> 15:20.187
Shadow!

15:25.393 --> 15:27.494
Oh, hey, is, uh...
is Dylan back here?

15:27.529 --> 15:29.830
Sorry, Brian, Dylan says he
doesn't want to see you anymore.

15:29.864 --> 15:31.465
We're not allowed
to let you in.

15:31.499 --> 15:33.300
Well, you know what?
I'm sorry, too.

15:33.335 --> 15:35.569
For what?
For this!

15:37.439 --> 15:39.473
Yeah, you know, you probably
would've had a better shot

15:39.507 --> 15:40.808
if you hadn't yelled
"For this!"

15:40.842 --> 15:42.176
You know,
that-that kind of telegraphed

15:42.210 --> 15:43.510
what you were gonna do.

15:43.545 --> 15:44.812
Ah, Stewie,
it's no use.

15:44.846 --> 15:46.747
They're never
gonna let us in.

15:46.781 --> 15:48.649
<i>Well, maybe
you can't get in,</i>

15:48.683 --> 15:51.151
but I think I know
someone who can.

15:53.555 --> 15:54.922
Zac Sawyer?

15:54.956 --> 15:57.358
I'm Zac Sawyer. Say "when."

15:57.392 --> 15:58.625
Acting.

15:58.660 --> 16:02.596
Singing: pop, classical,
light opera, Italian.

16:02.630 --> 16:06.066
Dancing: classical, tap, jazz.

16:06.101 --> 16:08.602
Horse riding.
Can I? Yes.

16:08.636 --> 16:10.437
Accents: 13.

16:10.472 --> 16:12.506
South American, South African,

16:12.540 --> 16:14.808
British upper class,
British Cockney.

16:14.843 --> 16:16.944
"What, Govnah?"
Somebody gonna stop me?

16:16.978 --> 16:18.545
Fencing, nunchucks.

16:18.580 --> 16:21.315
Driving?
Yes. Automatic and standard...

16:21.349 --> 16:22.716
Okay, okay. Wow.

16:22.751 --> 16:23.917
You're the worst.

16:23.952 --> 16:25.719
Welcome aboard.

16:29.424 --> 16:33.527
<i>We now return to The
Love Boat with Somali Pirates.</i>

16:33.561 --> 16:35.229
I am the captain now.

16:35.263 --> 16:36.797
Well, okay,
but that means

16:36.831 --> 16:39.533
you're in charge of getting
Mr. Devlin to admit

16:39.567 --> 16:41.035
that after 30 years,

16:41.069 --> 16:43.971
he still loves
Mrs. Devlin very much.

16:44.005 --> 16:46.473
That is not the captain's
responsibility!

16:46.508 --> 16:48.509
<i>Well, maybe not where
you come from,</i>

16:48.543 --> 16:50.344
but here on
the Love Boat...

16:51.679 --> 16:54.648
Now, who is Mr. Devlin?

16:54.682 --> 16:57.951
Try skinny-dipping with her
like when you were kids.

16:57.986 --> 16:59.920
Hey, Lois, if I was
gonna kill myself,

16:59.954 --> 17:02.489
do I slit my wrists
this way or this way?

17:02.524 --> 17:04.925
Sideways for attention,
long way for results!

17:04.959 --> 17:05.926
Meg is right.

17:05.960 --> 17:07.394
Okay, I'll be in
the garage.

17:07.429 --> 17:08.862
Peter, put that knife down.

17:08.897 --> 17:10.364
Lois, I don't
have any friends!

17:10.398 --> 17:11.565
So I'm gonna kill myself

17:11.599 --> 17:13.567
and get a full page
in the yearbook.

17:13.601 --> 17:15.302
<i>Then they can't
ignore me.</i>

17:15.336 --> 17:16.370
Who's that?

17:16.404 --> 17:18.005
I don't know.

17:21.943 --> 17:24.411
Okay, Zac, remember,
your character's name

17:24.446 --> 17:25.579
is Tevin Ashe-Taylor.

17:25.613 --> 17:27.414
You surf
and enjoy surfing.

17:27.449 --> 17:29.550
In this scene,
DJ Slopes is thinking

17:29.584 --> 17:31.051
of quitting
the detective agency,

17:31.086 --> 17:32.553
and you're like,
"No way."

17:32.587 --> 17:33.854
Got it.

17:34.989 --> 17:36.123
We're rolling!

17:36.157 --> 17:38.325
Listen, Tevin, I think
I just need more time

17:38.359 --> 17:39.860
to focus
on my snowboarding

17:39.894 --> 17:41.628
and Web series
about energy drinks.

17:41.663 --> 17:43.864
No, man, you got to stay
in the detective agency.

17:43.898 --> 17:46.300
It's what your dad
would want.

17:46.334 --> 17:48.268
What?
What are you talking about?

17:48.303 --> 17:49.736
What the hell's
that new kid doing?

17:49.771 --> 17:50.871
This isn't in the script.

17:50.905 --> 17:52.372
I'm talking about your dad, DJ.

17:52.407 --> 17:54.208
He sent me here with a message.

17:54.242 --> 17:57.211
He said he can't believe
he was so selfish.

17:57.245 --> 17:59.279
It's like you held
a mirror up to him

17:59.314 --> 18:02.483
and he could finally see how
poorly he'd been treating you.

18:02.517 --> 18:04.785
He didn't realize how lucky
he was...

18:04.819 --> 18:06.587
Stay on me,
camera one.

18:06.621 --> 18:08.789
...to have you
back in his life,

18:08.823 --> 18:12.192
and now he feels like
he's lost you all over again.

18:12.227 --> 18:15.329
But how do I know
he won't just hurt me again?

18:15.363 --> 18:17.464
You don't. You never know that.

18:17.499 --> 18:21.335
But isn't taking a risk
better than not having a dad?

18:21.369 --> 18:23.537
Yeah, I guess
you're right.

18:23.571 --> 18:26.039
You're damn right I am.

18:26.074 --> 18:27.407
Cut!

18:27.442 --> 18:29.576
You know, I don't know
where any of that came from,

18:29.611 --> 18:30.577
but I liked it.

18:30.612 --> 18:31.678
Who are you?

18:31.713 --> 18:33.280
I'm Zac Sawyer.

18:33.314 --> 18:35.082
And I go where I'm needed.

18:35.116 --> 18:39.853
♪ Here I go again on my own

18:39.888 --> 18:44.191
♪ Going down the only road
I've ever known. ♪

18:44.225 --> 18:45.759
Where you headed?

18:45.793 --> 18:47.194
Wherever I'm needed.

18:47.228 --> 18:48.962
No, no, it does
not work like that.

18:48.997 --> 18:50.697
I need to put
address in machine.

18:50.732 --> 18:53.133
Okay, maybe, you can
just take me home then.

18:53.168 --> 18:54.134
Where is home?

18:54.169 --> 18:55.569
I-I don't know.

18:55.603 --> 18:57.271
It's-it's my mom
and dad's house,

18:57.305 --> 18:58.872
but I don't
know the address!

18:58.907 --> 19:00.073
I take you airport.

19:00.108 --> 19:00.608
Okay!

19:04.979 --> 19:07.648
All right, Quagmire, I have
given this a lot of thought.

19:07.682 --> 19:09.783
I need you to shoot me
in the arm

19:09.817 --> 19:11.218
so we can be friends again.

19:11.252 --> 19:12.953
Okay.
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa!

19:12.987 --> 19:14.388
No, no, I was supposed
to offer,

19:14.422 --> 19:16.390
and then you were supposed
to say, "No, Peter,

19:16.424 --> 19:17.591
"I'm not gonna shoot you,

19:17.625 --> 19:19.059
although I do appreciate
the gesture."

19:19.093 --> 19:20.627
Hand me the gun, Peter.

19:20.662 --> 19:23.130
I, uh... I passed the test?

19:23.164 --> 19:25.899
No, Peter. I'm gonna shoot you
like a dirty animal.

19:25.934 --> 19:27.100
But-but just the fact

19:27.135 --> 19:29.336
that I would make the offer
is so meaningful to you.

19:29.370 --> 19:30.470
Your offer means
nothing, Peter.

19:30.505 --> 19:31.872
The only thing that
means anything to me

19:31.906 --> 19:33.607
is a bullet in your arm.
Now give me that!

19:33.641 --> 19:34.942
Stop! Stop!
Quagmire, let go!

19:34.976 --> 19:36.310
Come on! You're not leaving
here without being shot!

19:36.344 --> 19:37.678
Look, I was trying
to do the decent thing,

19:37.712 --> 19:38.946
like you'd see in a movie!

19:38.980 --> 19:40.180
I'm putting that bullet
in your arm, Peter!

19:40.215 --> 19:41.915
Ah! Son of a bitch!

19:41.950 --> 19:44.084
It's over.
We're all friends again.

19:44.118 --> 19:45.319
What the hell, Joe?!

19:45.353 --> 19:46.820
I'm just trying
to settle this nonsense.

19:46.854 --> 19:48.088
Nothing got settled!
You shot him!

19:48.122 --> 19:49.756
I'm the one who
has to shoot him!

19:49.791 --> 19:52.326
Wait a second. All right, then
I get to shoot Joe, all right?

19:52.360 --> 19:56.563
He shot me! I mean, if we're
doing things fair here, then...

19:56.598 --> 19:58.765
All right, we're good.

20:03.805 --> 20:04.771
Hey, Dad.

20:04.806 --> 20:05.772
Dylan?

20:05.807 --> 20:07.107
I went to your house,

20:07.141 --> 20:08.809
and Chris told me
you'd be here.

20:08.843 --> 20:11.545
Look, Dylan, I-I'm really sorry
I hurt you.

20:11.579 --> 20:15.048
I've been a really bad father,
like Ernest Hemmingway

20:15.083 --> 20:16.350
or-or some other great writer.

20:16.384 --> 20:18.118
But if-if you'll give me
a chance,

20:18.152 --> 20:20.148
I-I'd like
to try to change that.

20:20.149 --> 20:22.050
I'd like that, too, Dad.

20:34.830 --> 20:37.799
Peter, I'm so glad you patched
things up with your friends.

20:37.833 --> 20:40.776
Have I told you
that we're all friends again?

20:40.777 --> 20:43.366
Yes, Peter, we were just
talking about it.

20:43.367 --> 20:47.130
They're gonna take me
to the zoo and roll me around.

20:48.379 --> 20:50.347
We're all friends again.

20:50.348 --> 21:51.348
Thank you for watching «Family Guy» on
www.FOX-fan.ru